### AB

100K: zero-copy stream() 8.38M -> 8.71M (+3.9%), PATHS stream() 8.36M ->
8.33M (-0.4%), DBAPI 3.64M -> 3.64M (0.0%), core 3.61M -> 3.56M (-1.4%),
4 processes 8.83M -> 8.73M (-1.1%). The 100K read is unchanged within noise.

3K: zero-copy stream() 6.05M -> 6.76M (+11.7%), PATHS stream() 6.11M -> 6.52M
(+6.7%), DBAPI 7.34M -> 7.79M (+6.1%), core 4.91M -> 6.03M (+22.8%), 4
processes 3.93M -> 4.08M (+3.8%). The small-batch read is faster, i.e. below
baseline latency, in every fastpostgres path. This is the request shape where
the old per-batch mmap/munmap of the whole segment was a large share of the
work; caching the worker's mapping and never re-opening the segment removes
it.

## Correctness gate

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g fpq-formats tests.sh'
    -> Ran 54 tests ... OK (default /dev/shm path)

    FASTPOSTGRES_MEMFD=1 fpq-formats tests.sh
    -> 52/54 pass; the two failures inspect /dev/shm/<seg> directly
       (test_the_data_segment_capacity_is_the_clients_ask,
        test_parallel.test_threads_each_get_their_own_partition), which the
       memfd path deliberately does not create. The gate runs the suite without
       the opt-in env; a memfd-specific test is listed as follow-up in
       DESIGN.md.
